WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.625
Begin with the distinction between doing work and
waiting for a resource.

00:00:04.625 --> 00:00:09.250
Execution models estimate demand; queueing models
account for contention.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:15.206
Define the station boundary, number of servers and
capacity convention before using a formula.

00:00:15.206 --> 00:00:21.415
The same average service time can yield very
different response times when arrivals, scheduling
or

00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:23.125
competing workloads change.

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:26.069
Compute each metric with its units attached.

00:00:26.069 --> 00:00:32.557
Eight completions over twenty seconds means 0.4 jobs
per second, while sixteen busy seconds means

00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:34.363
eighty percent utilization.

00:00:34.363 --> 00:00:39.180
Operational laws connect these measurements when
their boundaries agree.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.735
Little’s law uses average jobs, throughput and
residence time; it is not restricted to exponential

00:00:45.735 --> 00:00:47.073
arrivals or service.

00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:52.264
M/M/1 provides a simple explanation for sharply
increasing delay near saturation.

00:00:52.264 --> 00:00:56.301
Its assumptions and stability condition are part of
the result.

00:00:56.301 --> 00:01:02.517
Birth–death balance gives state probabilities, while
additional servers or finite buffers require

00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:03.863
changed calculations.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:10.143
Distinguish average delay, queue-tail probability
and actual blocking rather than treating them as

00:01:10.143 --> 00:01:12.707
interchangeable measures of performance.
